awk: awk is utility designed for data extraction. Most of the time, it is used to extract fields from a file or an output.
===

Note: 
1. $0 means: The entire current line.
2. By default, awk treats whitespace as the field separator.
   Whitespace includes: Space, Multiple spaces, Tabs



ls -l | awk '{print $NF}'         --> to print last column
awk '/word/ {print}' filename     --> to search specific word
cat filename | awk '{$2="Replace_Word"; print $0}'  --> replace words 
awk 'length($0) > 15' filename     --> to print lines which has more than 15 characters
ls -l | awk '{print NF}'   --> to print no of coloumns
ls -l | awk '{if($9 == "Viswa") print $0;}'  --> print entire row if column 9 maches with name viswa.


# to remove duplicates in authorized_keys
awk '!seen[$0]++' authorized_keys > authorized_keys.tmp && mv authorized_keys.tmp authorized_keys


awk '{print $0}' awk_test.txt                      --> print entire line


awk '{print $1}' awk_test.txt                      --> print field 1 of every line


awk '{print $1, $2}' awk_test.txt                  --> Print multiple fields


awk -F ',' '{print $2}' awk_csv.txt                --> -F —> specify field separator , Use comma as the field separator


awk -F ':' '{print $1}' /etc/passwd                --> to know all usernames


awk -F ':' '{print $1, $6}' /etc/passwd            --> Get username and home directory


awk -F ':' '{print $1, $7}' /etc/passwd            --> Get username and shell


awk '$3 == "AWS" {print}' awk_test.txt             --> Suppose you want only people using AWS: Field 3 equals AWS.


awk '$3 == "AWS" {print $2, $4}' awk_test.txt      --> Print only specific fields for matching rows


awk '$3 != "Linux" {print $2}' awk_test.txt        --> != means not equal, This prints everyone whose skill is not Linux.


awk '$4 > 70000 {print $2, $4}' awk_test.txt       --> Numeric comparison, Find salaries greater than 70000


awk '$4 >= 70000 {print $2, $4}' awk_test.txt      --> Greater than or equal


awk '$4 < 70000 {print $2, $4}' awk_test.txt       --> Less than


awk '$4 <= 70000 {print $2, $4}' awk_test.txt      --> Less than or equal


awk '$4 > 60000 && $5 == "India" {print $2, $4}' awk_test.txt    -->  Multiple conditions — AND (&&) 


awk '$3 == "Linux" || $3 == "AWS" {print $2, $3}' awk_test.txt   --> OR (||)







